CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ not a pointer type: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ address is a pointer: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ pointer to it: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [dcl.type.decltype]/1.3: for an unparenthesized id-expression naming a
function, decltype is the function's type.  The front end had applied the
function-to-pointer conversion to the operand first and gave
decltype(add) the type int (*)(int, int), so `decltype(add) *p' was a
pointer to pointer (bin53: CONVERSION ERROR).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept
this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
